{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"f8b2345c-b3c1-486a-8d6c-8b6af5ab25e1","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I have resolved the issue by updating the HR target generator in [`gen.py`](file:///tmp/h-a5No1F/gen.py) and documented the changes in [`ANSWER.md`](file:///tmp/h-a5No1F/ANSWER.md).\n\n### Summary of Changes\n\n1. **[`gen.py`](file:///tmp/h-a5No1F/gen.py#L10-L13)**:\n   - Changed the lower bound key in [`hr_target`](file:///tmp/h-a5No1F/gen.py#L10-L13) from `\"value\"` to `\"start\"`, producing `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\n   - **Why**: Using `\"value\"` signals a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target during FIT export, which causes Garmin to clamp target ranges to its local zones. Changing the key to `\"start\"` produces an Instant-HR corridor, allowing Intervals.icu to export raw computed bpm targets matching the planned percentages.\n\n2. **[`ANSWER.md`](file:///tmp/h-a5No1F/ANSWER.md)**:\n   - Documented the exact code changes and underlying mechanism.\n   - Clarified which parts of the colleague's note applied (the `start` vs `value` key convention for Instant-HR targets) and which were ignored (the UI text-grammar `description` parser rules and external deployment/sync testing steps).\n","duration_seconds":36.35579,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":141746,"output_tokens":1486,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":0,"total_tokens":143232}}}
